`bytes.tokenizerId` — the `tokenizerId` property on the outcome returned by `submit(prompt)`.

The test (in `packages/core/test/cache.test.ts`, under the `Zone A — the frozen prefix (§10 rule 5, D5)` describe block) is:

```ts
it('records the injected tokenizer id on every outcome so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded range (§17)', () => {
  const heuristic = new ProviderCacheSimulator({ tokenizer: h.tokenizer }).submit(prompt);
  const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
    tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
  }).submit(prompt);

  expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
  expect(bytes.total).not.toBe(heuristic.total);
  expect(bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)).toEqual(heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds));
});
```

Note that `bytes` is the **submit outcome**, not the simulator instance — the test name states the design intent: the injected tokenizer id is recorded on *every outcome* so that swapping tokenizers is a deliberate, inspectable diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded token range. The neighboring assertions complete the picture: `bytes.total` must *differ* from the heuristic's (the byte-exact tokenizer counts differently), while the segment structure (`segments.map(s => s.blockIds)`) must be identical — same block segmentation, different token counts.

The property is **`tokenizerId`**.

The test — "records the injected tokenizer id on every outcome so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded range (§17)" — builds two simulators over the same prompt, one with the harness's heuristic tokenizer and one with `new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length)`, and asserts three things on the `bytes` outcome returned by `.submit(prompt)`:

```ts
expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
expect(bytes.total).not.toBe(heuristic.total);
expect(bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)).toEqual(heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds));
```

So `bytes.tokenizerId === 'bytes-v0'`: every submit outcome records which tokenizer produced it, so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate, visible diff in the recorded ranges rather than a silent shift — while the segment *structure* (the block-id partition) must be tokenizer-independent, which is what the final `segments` assertion pins.

